Virus Scanners: Every type and subtype of viruses have different propagation methods, and have different code structures. However, they all have similar features in thei
  codes, and virus scanners look for the signatures produced by malware. The virus scanner uses profiles to compare to files on the host device. If a file matches the signature
  in the profiles, the scanner will alert. Profiles contain general features for several types of malware, because viruses are constantly updating and so a specific profile
  might let too many viruses through. When it detects a mailcious file, it can either disinfect it (remove the malicious parts), put it in quarantine (where it can do no
  damage, but still attempt to be fixed) or deleted (once it has been proved to not harm the computer to do so).
  Source: https://www.gdatasoftware.com/guidebook/what-actually-is-a-virus-scanner
  Image Source: https://www.gdatasoftware.com/fileadmin/_processed_/e/2/G_DATA_Screenshot_IS_2018_SecurityCenter_EN_Correct_66ea191a5b.jpg
Hashcat: Hashcat is the fastest open-source password cracking software. This is important for security for a number of reasons. It can be used to test password strength.
  The quicker that it cracks the password, the weaker it is. This can prompt the user or the IT-department to create a new password, or strengthen the existing one. It 
  can be used to recover lost passwords when traditional password resets are impossible or impractical. Hashcat runs in a few ways. 
  Dictionary attack: This is when it uses a list of common passwords, dictionary words and pre-cracked passwords. It converts these words into hashes and checks against
    the desired hash. This is best for weak passwords.
  Brute-force attack: This is when it generates every character combination within a set of guidelines. It compares the hashes of these with the desired hash. It is the
    most computationally expensive though
  Rule-based attack: Similar to the dictionary attack, this swaps symbols in for letters of dictionary words following common password ruless. For example, 'a' becomes '@'.
  Source: https://jumpcloud.com/it-index/what-is-hashcat
  Image Source: https://hashcat.net/hashcat/hashcat700.png
